EXT. CORA'S APARTMENT - DRIVEWAY - MORNING

CORA VELASQUEZ, 30s, loads a garment bag into the trunk of an aging sedan. Her brother DANTE VELASQUEZ, 30s, waits at the curb with a single duffel, not quite meeting her eyes.

CORA
Fourteen hours to the venue if we don't stop much. Rehearsal dinner's at six tomorrow and I am not walking in late to Marisol's wedding because of you.

DANTE
I said I'd come. I'm here. Can we start the fourteen hours instead of narrating them?

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - DAY

The radio plays low. Cora keeps both hands on the wheel; Dante watches the mile markers pass like they owe him something.

DANTE
You didn't have to be the one to pick me up. Marisol offered.

CORA
Marisol has a wedding to run. I had a free Tuesday and a car. That's the whole reason.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - DAY (CONTINUOUS)

The silence stretches until Cora breaks it, eyes still on the road.

CORA
You weren't at Mom's funeral. Three years and you still haven't said one real word to me about that.

DANTE
There's no version of that sentence that doesn't sound like an excuse, Cora. So I mostly just didn't say anything.

CORA
An excuse would be better than nothing. Nothing is what I've been driving around with for three years.

EXT. GAS STATION - DAY

Dante pumps gas while Cora leans against the car, arms crossed, watching a family load into a minivan nearby.

DANTE
I'm not trying to fight the whole way there.

CORA
Then don't dodge the one question I actually asked you. That's not fighting, that's just answering.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - DAY

Cora digs a battered cassette out of the glovebox and holds it up without looking away from the road.

CORA
Found this cleaning out Mom's closet. "Summer 2004," in your handwriting. You made this for the drive to Aunt Renata's, remember?

DANTE
(a small, surprised laugh)
I forgot that even existed. Does the car even still have a tape deck?

CORA
It does not. I've just been carrying it around like it might fix itself into a CD eventually.

EXT. ROADSIDE DINER - DAY

Cora and Dante sit across a sticky table, plates mostly untouched between them.

DANTE
I wasn't at the funeral because I was in a hospital room four states away. I didn't want that to be the thing everyone talked about instead of Mom.

CORA
You could have told me that three years ago instead of letting me build every worse story in my head to fill in the blank.

DANTE
I know. I'm telling you now because I ran out of road to keep not telling you.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - AFTERNOON

An orange dashboard light blinks on. Cora eases the car onto the shoulder as it starts to shudder.

CORA
Perfect timing. Absolutely perfect.

DANTE
There's a shop marked on the map about two miles back. We passed a sign.

EXT. REPAIR SHOP - AFTERNOON

Cora and Dante sit on a bench outside the garage bay, watching a mechanic work under the hood.

DANTE
It was my heart. An arrhythmia thing, scarier than it sounds until it isn't. I didn't want to be the reason her funeral became about me instead of her.

CORA
It wouldn't have been about you. It would have been about both of us being there, which is a completely different thing than what actually happened.

DANTE
I know that now. I didn't know it then. I'm sorry it took three years and a broken alternator to say it out loud.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - EVENING

The car repaired, back on the road. Cora holds the cassette up again.

CORA
There's a gas station up ahead that still sells those little battery players. I saw one in a window sign an hour back.

DANTE
You want to actually listen to sixteen-year-old me's playlist right now?

CORA
I want to listen to it with you right now. There's a difference.

EXT. MOTEL - NIGHT

The car pulls into a motel lot, hours behind schedule, a vacancy sign buzzing red against the dark.

DANTE
We're not making it tonight, are we.

CORA
We're making it tomorrow morning instead. Marisol will survive one late arrival if it means we actually talk this out first.

INT. MOTEL ROOM - NIGHT

Two beds, a small speaker propped between them playing the cassette's tinny, hopeful pop songs. Cora and Dante lie on their backs, staring at the ceiling.

DANTE
You kept the tape three years and never once tried to play it.

CORA
I kept a lot of things three years and never tried much of anything with them. Guess that's the family trait we actually share.

DANTE
Tomorrow, at the wedding — I'd like to actually dance with my sister for once, if she'll let me.

CORA
Ask me again after the vows. I make no promises before coffee.

EXT. WEDDING VENUE - DAY

The car pulls up outside a flower-strung venue just as guests begin filing toward the entrance, Cora and Dante half-jogging across the gravel lot together.

DANTE
We made it. Barely, but we made it.

CORA
Barely counts. Barely is basically our whole family's love language.

INT. WEDDING VENUE - RECEPTION HALL - DAY

Later, string lights overhead. Cora and Dante stand at the edge of the dance floor, the old cassette playlist somehow, absurdly, part of the DJ's set now.

DANTE
(offering his hand)
You said ask again after the vows.

CORA
(taking it)
Vows are done. I suppose I did promise.

They step onto the floor together, the first dance either of them has shared in longer than either wants to admit out loud.
